@* The on/off switch itself: a track with a knob that slides across it. Purely the picture — the
   thing actually clicked is always the row or label around it, because all three of these carry a
   hint button or a caption that a real <button> could not contain.

   Three were spelled out by hand in the setup form and had to agree on five numbers at once
   (track 24×44, knob 20, its two offsets, and how far it travels). They already disagreed on
   more than that: the outcome switch is built on a peer checkbox and the two setup switches on a
   bool, so the same control was written two different ways a few hundred lines apart.

   Both shapes survive, because the difference is real: the outcome switch's state lives in a
   bound checkbox and is painted by CSS the instant it is ticked, with no round trip, while the
   setup rows have no checkbox and read their bool. What they now share is the geometry, which is
   the part that must never drift — hence the wrapper/fill/knob split, which the bool version did
   not need but is what lets the checkbox sit as a sibling of both painted layers. *@

<span class="relative inline-flex h-6 w-11 shrink-0 items-center">
    @* The peer checkbox, in the one place `peer-checked:` can reach both layers below it. *@
    @ChildContent
    <span class="absolute inset-0 rounded-full transition-colors @Fill"></span>
    <span class="absolute left-0.5 top-0.5 h-5 w-5 rounded-full bg-white shadow transition-transform @Slide"></span>
</span>

@code {
    /// <summary>Whether it is on. Ignored when a peer checkbox is supplied — that says instead.</summary>
    [Parameter] public bool On { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The bound <c>peer</c> checkbox, for a switch whose state is a checkbox rather than a bool
    /// — it then flips under the finger instead of after the server answers. Pass the
    /// <c>peer-checked:</c> form of <see cref="OnClass"/> along with it.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public RenderFragment? ChildContent { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The class that paints the track on — jade unless the switch belongs to something amber.
    /// With a peer checkbox, pass the <c>peer-checked:</c> form of it, spelled out: Tailwind scans
    /// the source for literal class names, so a variant this component composed would never be
    /// emitted. (The variant also outranks the off fill on specificity, which is why both can sit
    /// on the element at once.)
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public string OnClass { get; set; } = "bg-accent-500";

    private bool Peered => ChildContent is not null;

    private string Fill => Peered ? $"bg-ink-200 {OnClass}" : On ? OnClass : "bg-ink-200";

    private string Slide => Peered ? "peer-checked:translate-x-5" : On ? "translate-x-5" : "";
}
